[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  friday july 1st i'm julie chang for the  wall street journal filling in for zoe  thomas  elon musk and twitter have been in talks  about a 44 billion dollar acquisition  since april so three months in what's  the holdup the tesla and spacex ceo has  said there's three issues standing in  the way lining up financing for the deal  winning shareholder approval and bots he  thinks twitter is rife with fake  accounts so much so that he put the deal  on hold while demanding the company turn  over data on its fake or spam accounts  now some have speculated that the move  was an attempt at renegotiating or  scuttling the deal altogether whatever  the case in recent weeks musk got that  data  but how useful was it to him really and  where does the deal go next here to help  answer those questions is wsj tech  reporter sarah needleman who's been  following the story hi sarah thanks for  coming on the show hi thanks for having  me  so elon musk got what he asked for from  twitter he got this huge data dump  exactly how much data are we talking  here so he received historical tweak  data and access to the company's  so-called fire hose of tweets uh the  fire hose shows the full flood of tweets  and keep in mind that people post about  hundreds of millions of times a day on  the platform according to the company  and he's accessing this data in near  real time so it's a large volume of  information and what was included in  that data  it's really just tweets and the  timestamps and dates of when those  tweets were put out so there aren't any  direct messages for example dms it  doesn't include any personal or private  information from users like their ip  addresses or email addresses  so it's really just tweets and their  timestamps  since it's just tweets and time stamps  can that information actually be used to  identify bots and if so i mean how  accurate would that be well you could  use it to find  some bots what some of the experts i  spoke to say they described it as low  hanging fruit so these would be obvious  instances of bots just by you know  looking through the data certainly if a  particular account is tweeting more  times in a short period than a human  possibly could that's a good indication  of a bot but it also could dig up bots  that are arguably useful um ones that  just post say weather alerts or photos  of cue animals it would miss  sophisticated human-like bot behavior  because you really need some of that  personal data to cross-check to make  sure a user is human or not and these  bots are very sophisticated and so to go  through at least in a short period of  time because we know that shareholders  twitter shareholders are voting on the  deal  later this summer so he is strapped for  time to go through it and parse that  data to figure out which accounts maybe  bots and not just bots in general but  ones that are spewing spam as opposed to  like i said helpful information like  weather alerts  so that actually raises another question  how do you determine what's considered a  fake or spam account or not right  there's no standard industry definition  so  if mr musk and twitter were to compare  say just analysis of the fire hose data  and nothing else and keep it more of an  apples to apples comparison they would  have to agree on what constitutes a bad  bot or malicious behavior and come  across some sort of a universal  definition  and this goes back to the point they're  saying that when twitter comes up with  this estimate it does have access to  that personal data we talked about  earlier and that's something mr musk  doesn't have and critter also uses  humans you know employees to analyze  the fire hose data and that's something  mr musk wouldn't be able to replicate so  the comparison to twitter's  process for estimating  spam on its platform isn't something  that mr musk or really anyone could  easily replicate so elon musk got this  data what does that mean for the deal  because he's been saying that he thinks  the bots make up about 20 of accounts  twitter has said they only make up five  percent of accounts so would this  discrepancy be enough grounds for him to  pull out of the deal  he could try but again we he has to come  up with a  number and share the process for coming  up with it and making convincing  argument that whatever he finds is a  perhaps a more accurate number or that  twitter was either dishonest or just did  a poor job of assessing  its platform and again that's very  difficult to do when you can't compare  to their process that's done internally  another factor considers that twitter's  number is based on its population of  monetizable daily users  and we also know that many people log on  to twitter just to reposts they don't  actually tweet themselves  you know that's a significant share of  the platform's users and that points to  another limitation of the fire hose data  because that could meaningly affect how  percentages of users are calculated  you know so the experts i spoke to say  it would be surprising that if he comes  up with a different number it may not be  enough to change the course of the deal  or the terms of the deal and what  happens next in the deal you mentioned  that there's going to be a vote later in  the summer is there anything else that  we can look forward to  uh that is a major next step but we're  talking about someone who has surprised  us many instances over these last few  months  so we have to see if mr musk will come  forward and say that his team has come  up with a different number and perhaps  he will argue that  twitter's process was faulty  but again it's difficult to know exactly  whether or not that would be enough to  change the course of the deal given the  factors we talked about earlier  so it remains to be seen but the next  step that we know about  being on the books or being on the  record is this upcoming shareholder vote  so between now and then it's anyone's  guess as to what mr musk might say  at an event or on twitter itself  speaking of which mr musk hasn't tweeted  since june 21st so as of  thursday that would be nine days and  pretty unusual for mr musk who is known  to be a serial tweeter and the fact that  he's been quiet is very interesting or  it's at least somewhat surprising so all  eyes are on  that platform to see what he says next  and when he says it next we're talking  about a person who's known for making  news based on a single tweet and now  we're saying there's news in the fact  that he hasn't tweeted um is in itself  also pretty interesting i agree that is  pretty interesting all right that was  our tech reporter sarah needleman sarah  thanks so much for being here thanks for  having me  and that's it for tech news briefing  this week we had production assistants  from ariana aspuru we had editorial  support from scott salloway are  supervising producer is chris zinsley  our executive producer is kateri yokum  and i'm your host julie chang filling in  for zoe thomas a quick programming note  will be off on monday for independence  day but we'll be back in your feed on  tuesday thanks for listening and have a  great weekend  [Music]  [Music]  you

